# Target C-II — simultaneous reflection modules admit disjoint growing contact buffers

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only strengthening of the simultaneous reflection block-diagonal reduction; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the controlled slow-scale `eta=3/26` reflection family  
**Dependencies:** simultaneous reflection block diagonalization; source unit-contact degree bound  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_tooth_reflection_growing_contact_buffers_2026_09_17.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The simultaneous reflection theorem gives pairwise-separated blocker modules

\[
U_i=\{z\in A:|z-q_i|>D\}
\]

with

\[
\left|\bigcup_iU_i\right|
\le(C_0+o(1)){D\over\kappa_D},
\qquad
C_0=1.59653882460\ldots,
\tag{CB.1}
\]

and

\[
\operatorname{dist}(U_i,U_j)
=\Omega(\kappa_DD^{3/4})
\qquad(i\ne j).
\tag{CB.2}
\]

This note propagates that separation through the original unit-contact graph. Because a unit-separated planar source has unit-contact degree at most six, a graph ball of radius `r` contains at most order `5^r` vertices per seed. The blocker union is already `O(D/kappa_D)`, so one may take a contact radius tending to infinity while keeping the full contact buffer sublinear.

Set

\[
\boxed{
r_D:=\left\lfloor{1\over2}\log_5\kappa_D\right\rfloor.}
\tag{CB.3}
\]

Then `r_D->infinity`, and if `N_r(S)` denotes the closed radius-`r` neighborhood of a vertex set `S` in the original source unit-contact graph,

\[
\boxed{
\left|N_{r_D}\left(\bigcup_iU_i\right)\right|
\le
(2.39480823691\ldots+o(1)){D\over\sqrt{\kappa_D}}
=o(D).
}
\tag{CB.4}
\]

Moreover the individual neighborhoods

\[
\mathcal C_i:=N_{r_D}(U_i)
\tag{CB.5}
\]

are pairwise disjoint, and there is no unit-contact edge between `C_i` and `C_j` for `i!=j`. Thus every blocker module is surrounded by a **diverging contact-graph buffer** before different modules can interact.

A useful explicit polynomial choice is

\[
\kappa_D=D^{1/8}.
\tag{CB.6}
\]

It retains

\[
(\rho_*-o(1))D^{1/8}
\tag{CB.7}
\]

simultaneous reflection modules, while

\[
\boxed{
r_D=\left({1\over16}+o(1)\right)\log_5D}
\tag{CB.8}
\]

and their entire disjoint buffered union has size

\[
\boxed{O(D^{15/16})=o(D).}
\tag{CB.9}
\]

So the local-repair obstruction cannot be blamed on immediate contact entanglement between different reflection modules. Even after exposing a logarithmically growing number of contact layers around each blocker set, the modules remain disjoint and together consume only sublinear source mass.

---

## 1. The source unit-contact graph has maximum degree six

Let `G_1` be the graph on the source points with an edge for every pair at distance exactly one. Fix a source point `v`. All contact neighbors lie on the unit circle centered at `v`. If two distinct neighbors subtend angle `theta`, their mutual distance is

\[
2\sin(\theta/2).
\]

Source separation requires this to be at least one, so

\[
\theta\ge\frac\pi3.
\]

At most six such directions fit around a full angle. Therefore

\[
\boxed{\Delta(G_1)\le6.}
\tag{CB.10}
\]

This is source-wide and does not use Type II.

---

## 2. Contact balls grow by at most a factor five per additional layer

In any graph of maximum degree six, a closed radius-`r` ball around one vertex contains at most

\[
1+6\sum_{j=0}^{r-1}5^j
=1+{6(5^r-1)\over4}
={3\cdot5^r-1\over2}
\tag{CB.11}
\]

vertices. The factor after the first layer is five because a shortest-path tree vertex has at most five children after excluding its parent.

For any seed set `S`, union bounding the balls gives

\[
\boxed{
|N_r(S)|
\le {3\cdot5^r-1\over2}|S|.
}
\tag{CB.12}
\]

No disjointness assumption is required for this upper bound.

---

## 3. A diverging contact radius still costs only `o(D)` vertices

Apply (CB.12) to

\[
S_D:=\bigcup_iU_i.
\]

The controlled simultaneous theorem gives (CB.1). For `r_D` from (CB.3),

\[
5^{r_D}\le\sqrt{\kappa_D}.
\tag{CB.13}
\]

Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
|N_{r_D}(S_D)|
&\le
{3\cdot5^{r_D}-1\over2}|S_D|\\
&\le
\left({3C_0\over2}+o(1)\right){D\over\sqrt{\kappa_D}}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{CB.14}
\]

Numerically,

\[
\boxed{{3C_0\over2}=2.39480823690\ldots .}
\tag{CB.15}
\]

Since `kappa_D->infinity`, this is `o(D)`. At the same time `r_D->infinity`.

More generally, any integer radius satisfying

\[
5^{r_D}=o(\kappa_D)
\tag{CB.16}
\]

keeps the full buffered blocker union sublinear.

---

## 4. Different module buffers remain disjoint

Every contact edge has Euclidean length one. Therefore the graph distance between two vertex sets is at least their Euclidean distance:

\[
\operatorname{dist}_{G_1}(S,T)
\ge\operatorname{dist}_{\mathbb R^2}(S,T).
\tag{CB.17}
\]

For distinct blocker modules, the simultaneous theorem gives

\[
\operatorname{dist}_{\mathbb R^2}(U_i,U_j)
=\Omega(\kappa_DD^{3/4}).
\tag{CB.18}
\]

But

\[
r_D=O(\log\kappa_D)
=o(\kappa_DD^{3/4}).
\tag{CB.19}
\]

If `C_i=N_{r_D}(U_i)` and `C_j=N_{r_D}(U_j)` intersected, there would be a contact path from `U_i` to `U_j` of length at most `2r_D`. If a unit-contact edge joined `C_i` to `C_j`, there would be such a path of length at most `2r_D+1`. Both contradict (CB.18)--(CB.19) for sufficiently large `D`.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal C_i\cap\mathcal C_j=\varnothing,
\qquad
E_{G_1}(\mathcal C_i,\mathcal C_j)=\varnothing
\quad(i\ne j).
}
\tag{CB.20}
\]

This is stronger than the blocker-set disjointness itself: the separation survives a graph radius tending to infinity.

---

## 5. Explicit polynomial tradeoff

Choose

\[
\kappa_D=D^\beta,
\qquad
0<\beta<\frac14.
\tag{CB.21}
\]

Then the slow-scale extraction retains

\[
(\rho_*-o(1))D^{1/4-\beta}
\tag{CB.22}
\]

modules. With the half-logarithmic buffer choice,

\[
r_D
=\left({\beta\over2}+o(1)\right)\log_5D,
\tag{CB.23}
\]

and

\[
\left|\bigcup_i\mathcal C_i\right|
=O(D^{1-\beta/2}).
\tag{CB.24}
\]

The Euclidean separation between blocker modules is

\[
\Omega(D^{3/4+\beta}),
\tag{CB.25}
\]

far larger than the logarithmic graph radius.

At the balanced illustrative choice `beta=1/8`,

\[
|I_D|=(\rho_*-o(1))D^{1/8},
\qquad
r_D=\left({1\over16}+o(1)\right)\log_5D,
\tag{CB.26}
\]

and

\[
\left|\bigcup_i\mathcal C_i\right|
=O(D^{15/16}).
\tag{CB.27}
\]

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The reflection branch now has simultaneous modules that are independent on two scales:

1. metric independence: cross-module reflected-apex/blocker pairs lie below `D` by `Omega(kappa_D^2 sqrt D)`;
2. contact independence: the unit-contact neighborhoods of radius `r_D->infinity` around different blocker modules are disjoint and have no cross edges.

For `kappa_D=D^(1/8)`, polynomially many modules survive while each has a logarithmically growing isolated contact buffer, and all buffers together still occupy only `o(D)` vertices.

This still does not produce a legal repair. Translating a finite contact neighborhood can shorten unit contacts at its outer boundary, and the theorem does not show that a whole contact component is sublinear or bounded. The next target is now sharper: exploit the thin-shell path structure inside `U_i` together with the growing isolated buffer to prove either

- a contact component terminates inside the growing buffer, permitting a rigid translation/rotation repair; or
- a contact chain survives through `r_D` layers, in which case its forced geometric displacement/order must contradict the narrow antipodal module geometry.

**Disposition:** enlarged every diagonal blocker module to a contact radius tending to infinity while preserving pairwise module separation and keeping the total buffered population sublinear. C-II and Level I remain open.
